# Knowing the Mean Is Not Predicting the Next Star

Open index.html directly, or serve it alongside TeachingTools. This is native
HTML/Canvas with local seeded simulation and analytic Normal inference.
No R, Shiny, WebR or network access is needed at runtime.
The existing app.R/model.R remain unchanged as scientific references.

Lecture: https://anguswright.github.io/LectureNotes/IntroductionToStatistics_Section3c_html.html

## Model and interpretation

The simulated true population mean is 2 km/s. Stellar velocities have known
intrinsic SD tau; measurements add independent noise with known SD sigma.
The proper mean prior is Normal(0,5^2). Given n observations x, let
S=tau^2+sigma^2, V=1/(1/25+n/S), and m=V*sum(x)/S.
The three Normal distributions share mean m but have variances:

- Population mean: V.
- Next latent stellar velocity: V+tau^2.
- Next measured velocity: V+tau^2+sigma^2.

More data reduce V, not the irreducible future scatter. Rows are independently
peak-scaled to compare widths; their shaded areas/heights are not comparable
probability masses. Each central interval contains 95% under its own
distribution. Only the mean interval is a parameter credible interval.

## Intentional differences from R

- The model, defaults, Normal prior and nested 300-star design are unchanged.
- Astro.createRng(seed) replaces R's generator, so random realisations are not
  bitwise identical to R. Increasing n uses prefixes of the same observations.
  Dispersion changes transform the same standard-Normal draws; hiding data
  and resizing do not change observations. There is no gratuitous animation.
- Intervals use qnorm(.975)=1.9599639845... rather than R's plotted 1.96.
  This small accuracy improvement is also disclosed in the Explain tab.
- Added observation and analytic SD-versus-n plots, a variance/interval table
  and a display-only data-visibility control. Seed range is 0..2147483647.
- Known dispersions, certain membership and no selection are retained
  assumptions, not estimated quantities or silently added uncertainties.

## Files and exports

Runtime: index.html, model.js, app.js, and shared TeachingTools/statistics.js,
browser.js, lesson.js, browser.css. No additional runtime assets.
Pure CommonJS/browser global PosteriorPredictiveModel exports:
predictiveNormal(x, intrinsic, measurement, priorSd=5) and compute(parameters).
The posterior includes mean, named mean/star/measurement variances, exact
central 95% intervals and observationVariance.
compute parameters: seed, n, intrinsic, measurement, show_data.
Expected invalid settings throw Astro.ValidationError.

## Validation

From the repository root, with Node 22+ and Chrome:

    node PosteriorPredictive/test_model.js
    mkdir -p PosteriorPredictive/.browser-profile
    env -u HTML_SITE_ROOT TMPDIR="$PWD/PosteriorPredictive/.browser-profile" node PosteriorPredictive/test_browser.js
    rmdir PosteriorPredictive/.browser-profile

Numerical tests preserve the test_numerics.R oracle for x=[1,2,3], tau=1,
sigma=2: mean=1.875; variances=[1.5625,2.5625,6.5625]. They also check
independent retained-R/qnorm values, large-n irreducible variance differences,
zero intrinsic scatter, invalid data and nested fixed-seed observations.

    Rscript -e 'source("PosteriorPredictive/model.R"); print(predictive_normal(c(1,2,3),1,2))'

Browser tests inspect all three real density colours, exact equality of mean
and latent-star intervals at zero intrinsic scatter, correct predictive floors,
observations surviving n/display changes and small-noise boundaries, plus the
shared presets/reset, seed validation, keyboard tabs, mobile and offline-file
checks.
